WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.480
All right.

00:04.480 --> 00:10.480
So what is exactly having you can use it in order to filter your data, but after the aggregation.

00:10.480 --> 00:15.600
So that means we can use the having only after using the groupby.

00:15.640 --> 00:17.120
So let's see the syntax of that.

00:17.120 --> 00:21.280
So again like the previous example we are finding the total score by country.

00:21.280 --> 00:23.720
So we have our select from groupby.

00:23.760 --> 00:26.920
And now you say you know what I would like to filter the end results.

00:26.920 --> 00:30.760
And in order to do that we use the having after the group I.

00:30.800 --> 00:33.960
And now like the where clause you have to specify a condition.

00:33.960 --> 00:37.240
So we have the following condition where we want to see the results.

00:37.240 --> 00:42.000
Only the countries if their total score is higher than 800.

00:42.000 --> 00:43.840
So this can be our condition.

00:43.840 --> 00:46.240
So now you might noticing something with the groupby.

00:46.280 --> 00:50.480
We are using the country, the column where we are grouping the data by its value.

00:50.480 --> 00:55.640
But with the having we are using the aggregated column the sum of the score.

00:55.680 --> 00:57.520
So this is how the syntax works.

00:57.520 --> 00:59.400
And now let's see how SQL can execute it.

00:59.400 --> 01:01.440
So as usual SQL start with the from.

01:01.440 --> 01:02.800
We are getting our data.

01:02.800 --> 01:07.640
And then the second step is going to go and aggregate the data by the country.

01:07.640 --> 01:12.240
So it's like before it's going to group the rows with the same value of the country.

01:12.240 --> 01:14.600
So we're going to have one row for each country.

01:14.600 --> 01:16.720
And this is what's going to happen if you use groupby.

01:16.720 --> 01:19.120
And with that we have now aggregated values.

01:19.120 --> 01:19.560
Right.

01:19.560 --> 01:23.360
And after the groupby SQL is going to go and execute the halving.

01:23.360 --> 01:25.120
So halving it is like a filter.

01:25.160 --> 01:26.560
Now we have a nice condition.

01:26.560 --> 01:29.040
The total sale must be higher than 800.

01:29.080 --> 01:32.520
And SQL can go and check the new results after the aggregation.

01:32.520 --> 01:35.360
So in Germany we have the total sales of 850.

01:35.400 --> 01:38.320
So it meets the condition and it's going to stay in the results.

01:38.320 --> 01:39.680
The same thing for USA.

01:39.720 --> 01:41.720
It is higher as well than 900.

01:41.760 --> 01:45.080
But for UK it is not meeting the condition 750.

01:45.120 --> 01:46.800
It is not higher than 800.

01:46.880 --> 01:49.840
And SQL is going to go and filter out this row.

01:49.840 --> 01:55.160
So that means after applying the halving, we will get only two countries because they have values that

01:55.160 --> 01:57.280
is fulfilling the condition and that's it.

01:57.280 --> 01:59.480
This is what can happen if you are using halving.

01:59.520 --> 02:01.130
It is simply filtering the data.

02:01.130 --> 02:02.450
But now you might be confused.

02:02.450 --> 02:03.690
You say, you know what?

02:03.690 --> 02:06.090
We have used the Where clause to filter the data.

02:06.090 --> 02:09.530
So why we have in SQL another clause to filter my data.

02:09.570 --> 02:11.090
Can't we just use the where?

02:11.130 --> 02:15.890
Well, in SQL there are like different ways on how to filter your data based on the scenario.

02:15.890 --> 02:17.850
So now let's go and add both of the filters.

02:17.850 --> 02:18.770
In my query.

02:18.810 --> 02:21.250
We are already using the having after the group P.

02:21.290 --> 02:23.210
And now let's go and add the where.

02:23.250 --> 02:28.170
Usually the where comes between the from and the group by so directly after the from.

02:28.170 --> 02:31.130
And here we are saying the score must be higher than 400.

02:31.130 --> 02:33.810
So now we are filtering based on the scores twice, right?

02:33.810 --> 02:36.290
Once we are saying the score higher than 400.

02:36.290 --> 02:40.610
And by having we are saying the sum of score must be higher than 800.

02:40.610 --> 02:42.250
So what is the big difference?

02:42.250 --> 02:44.210
It is when the filter is happening.

02:44.210 --> 02:49.490
If you want to filter the data before the aggregation, you want to filter the original data.

02:49.530 --> 02:52.090
Then you can go and use the Where clause.

02:52.090 --> 02:57.930
But if you want to filter the data after the aggregations after the group, by then you can go and use

02:57.930 --> 02:58.410
having.

02:58.410 --> 03:01.370
So it's really all about when the filter is happening.

03:01.370 --> 03:03.170
So let's see how SQL can execute this.

03:03.170 --> 03:06.570
So as usual first the form can be executed to get the data.

03:06.610 --> 03:09.930
Then after that the second step the where going to be executed.

03:09.930 --> 03:11.570
This is our first filter.

03:11.570 --> 03:17.610
So SQL can filter the data using where before doing any aggregations and based on our condition, the

03:17.610 --> 03:23.650
first customer will be filtered out because score is less than 400 and the same thing for the last customer.

03:23.690 --> 03:28.850
Now after the applying the Where clause, we will get only three rows, only three customers.

03:28.890 --> 03:31.610
And now next SQL going to go and execute the group by.

03:31.650 --> 03:34.450
So it's going to go and group the data by the country.

03:34.450 --> 03:36.530
So now we have fewer data to be combined.

03:36.530 --> 03:40.450
So the values will not be summarized because we have only one row for each country.

03:40.490 --> 03:47.050
Now after the data is aggregated by the group by then SQL can activate the second filter having.

03:47.050 --> 03:49.290
So the next step is to execute the having.

03:49.330 --> 03:52.890
And here SQL is going to filter the new results based on the total scores.

03:52.930 --> 03:54.650
And SQL can check one by one.

03:54.650 --> 04:00.220
So USA is meeting the condition UK is going to be filtered out because it is not higher than 800 and

04:00.220 --> 04:05.820
this time Germany as well will be filtered out because this time it is not fulfilling the condition.

04:05.820 --> 04:10.900
In the previous example, without the where we had more scores for Germany, that's why it passed the

04:10.900 --> 04:11.180
test.

04:11.180 --> 04:16.100
But this time, since we filtered a lot of customers using the where, Germany will not have enough

04:16.100 --> 04:18.100
scores but the second filter.

04:18.100 --> 04:21.100
So with that in the output we will get only one row.

04:21.140 --> 04:23.220
And that's because we are filtering a lot of data.

04:23.220 --> 04:24.500
So it is very simple.

04:24.500 --> 04:29.660
We are going to be executed before the groupby, before the aggregations, having going to be executed

04:29.700 --> 04:31.940
after the group by after the aggregations.

04:31.940 --> 04:34.580
So now let's go back to SQL in order to practice okay.

04:34.620 --> 04:36.220
So now we have very interesting task.

04:36.260 --> 04:42.540
Find the average score for each country considering only customers with a score not equal to zero.

04:42.540 --> 04:49.220
So it sounds like condition and return only those countries with an average score greater than 430.

04:49.220 --> 04:51.300
So this is again another condition.

04:51.300 --> 04:53.620
So I know there is a lot of things that's going on.

04:53.620 --> 04:55.060
Let's do it step by step.

04:55.100 --> 05:00.260
Usually I start by doing a very simple Select statement with the columns and data that I need.

05:00.260 --> 05:02.100
So let's start with a simple select.

05:02.100 --> 05:03.140
So what do we need over here.

05:03.140 --> 05:04.020
We need a score.

05:04.020 --> 05:07.460
We need a country again we need a score country.

05:07.460 --> 05:08.940
So all what we need is two columns.

05:08.980 --> 05:12.420
Now I'm going to go and select the ID just to see the customer ID.

05:12.460 --> 05:16.460
Then let's go and get the country score from our table customers.

05:16.460 --> 05:17.660
So let's go and query that.

05:17.660 --> 05:22.180
So now as you can see I start with the basics query the data and then built up on top of it.

05:22.180 --> 05:22.980
The second step.

05:22.980 --> 05:24.140
Now what do we have in the task.

05:24.140 --> 05:27.220
We have to find the average score for each country.

05:27.220 --> 05:29.220
That means we have to do some aggregations.

05:29.220 --> 05:30.580
And here we have two conditions.

05:30.580 --> 05:35.260
The first condition says we need only the customers with a score not equal to zero.

05:35.260 --> 05:40.100
And the second one, we need only the countries with an average score greater than 430.

05:40.140 --> 05:45.100
Now you have to decide for each condition whether you're going to use the where or having.

05:45.140 --> 05:48.860
Now for the first one, we want to filter based on the scores.

05:48.860 --> 05:52.340
So that means we want to filter before the aggregations.

05:52.340 --> 05:55.460
It's not saying the average score, it's saying the score itself.

05:55.460 --> 05:58.700
So that means we can use for this a aware condition.

05:58.740 --> 05:59.860
Now about the second one.

05:59.860 --> 06:03.460
It says countries with an average score greater than 430.

06:03.500 --> 06:07.380
That means we want to filter the data after aggregating the score.

06:07.380 --> 06:10.700
So that means for this condition we have to use the having.

06:10.700 --> 06:14.180
Now, what I would like to do is to implement the first condition.

06:14.180 --> 06:14.820
It's very simple.

06:14.820 --> 06:19.940
We're going to say where after the from the score is not equal to zero.

06:19.940 --> 06:21.500
So let's go and execute it.

06:21.540 --> 06:26.300
And with that we don't have any customers where the scores is not equal to zero.

06:26.300 --> 06:28.260
So that we have solved this part.

06:28.300 --> 06:29.860
But now for the second condition.

06:29.860 --> 06:31.460
First we have to do the aggregations.

06:31.460 --> 06:33.860
So we're going to start with the average score.

06:33.860 --> 06:36.060
We're going to go over here and say average.

06:36.060 --> 06:38.060
And we're going to call it average score.

06:38.700 --> 06:40.820
Now we don't want to see only the average score.

06:40.820 --> 06:43.380
We want to see the average score for each country.

06:43.380 --> 06:46.060
So that means we have to aggregate by the country.

06:46.060 --> 06:47.940
And for that we use the groupby.

06:47.980 --> 06:50.820
GroupBy comes always after the where clause.

06:50.820 --> 06:53.740
So group by and which column it's going to be the country.

06:53.740 --> 06:54.900
So country.

06:54.940 --> 06:56.260
Now there is like an issue here.

06:56.260 --> 06:57.990
You cannot execute it like this.

06:58.030 --> 06:59.950
We have to go and get rid of the ID.

06:59.990 --> 07:03.270
We don't need it at all, so let's go and execute it.

07:03.270 --> 07:08.310
So with that, we have the average score for each country and we have solved the first part.

07:08.310 --> 07:11.710
So that means the first and the second parts they are completed.

07:11.750 --> 07:13.790
Now we're going to talk about the last part.

07:13.830 --> 07:16.790
The average score must be higher than 430.

07:16.830 --> 07:18.390
And for that we're going to use the halving.

07:18.390 --> 07:20.550
And halving comes after the groupby.

07:20.950 --> 07:22.710
Now we need to specify the condition.

07:22.710 --> 07:24.670
It must be the aggregated column.

07:24.670 --> 07:28.750
So we're going to take the average score from here and put it after the halving.

07:28.750 --> 07:31.470
And it should be greater than 430.

07:31.510 --> 07:32.350
So that's it.

07:32.390 --> 07:34.350
With that we have the last part as well.

07:34.350 --> 07:36.430
Let's go and execute it now.

07:36.430 --> 07:40.630
And with that my friends we have filter the data after the aggregation.

07:40.630 --> 07:43.630
So this is how I decide between the where and having.

07:43.670 --> 07:44.750
It is very simple.

07:44.790 --> 07:45.190
All right.

07:45.190 --> 07:49.230
So with that you have learned how to filter the aggregated data using the halving.

07:49.270 --> 07:54.630
And now next we're going to go back to the top where we can use there the keyword distinct exactly after

07:54.630 --> 07:55.390
the select.

07:55.390 --> 07:57.790
So let's go now and learn about the distinct.
